 ## Overview
-Pulsar is an efficient, sphere-based differentiable renderer that maps a 3D scene representation of spheres to a feature image, enabling end-to-end optimization of scene geometry, features, and camera parameters [S1]. It solves the problem of slow rendering and topological inconsistencies inherent in mesh-based or voxel-based neural rendering by using a sparse, explicit sphere representation that naturally culls empty space without acceleration structures [S1]. Unlike its siblings, Pulsar parallelizes over pixels rather than spheres to avoid synchronization overhead, and it decouples geometry projection from appearance via a neural shading network, allowing unlimited spheres to contribute to color while restricting gradient propagation to the first few spheres to avoid high-frequency artifacts [S1].
+Pulsar is an efficient, sphere-based differentiable renderer that maps a 3D scene representation of spheres to a feature image, enabling end-to-end optimization of scene geometry, features, and camera parameters [S1]. It solves the problem of slow rendering and topological inconsistencies inherent in mesh-based or voxel-based neural rendering by using a sparse, explicit sphere representation that naturally culls empty space without acceleration structures [S1]. Unlike its siblings, Pulsar parallelizes over pixels rather than spheres to avoid synchronization overhead, and it decouples geometry projection from appearance via a neural shading network, allowing unlimited spheres to contribute to color while restricting gradient propagation to the first few spheres to avoid high-frequency artifacts [S1]. It builds on [Differentiable Point-Based Radiance Fields](7366-differentiable-point-based-radiance-fields.md).
 
